---
name: sdh-visual-mockups
description: >
  Use when designing a non-server visual surface — a new screen, landing page,
  component, or redesign. Mockups via any visual preview available to the agent
  (artifacts / HTML / rendered images), variants side by side, pixel-precise
  iteration to acceptance. The accepted mockup is the design gate: it feeds the
  spec (phase-workflow) and usually enables the autonomous mode
  (release-lifecycle).
---

A feature with a visual surface, where the main decision is how it looks. The accepted mockup carries the design decisions and is the design gate. Not for server or non-visual work.

## Show, don't describe

Any visual preview the agent has (artifact, HTML in a browser, rendered images).
- 2–4 variants side by side; in an interactive medium, toggles for variant, light/dark theme and width.
- Iterate on concrete edits ("smaller gap", "chip further left").
- Full width if the product is full width — no artificial `max-width`.
- A failure or refusal frame shows every surface the failure changes (list row, draft tile, composer), not only the message.

## Ground the fork in what exists

- Before drawing, grep how this kind of element is already drawn in the project and include it, labelled "how it looks today" — a screenshot taken this session, not a redraw from memory. No such element yet → say so: "both variants are new".
- The owner's pointer to a donor ("like in X") is taken from every visible property of the donor's element: shape, colour in each theme, size, states (normal / negative / hover). List those properties on the board next to the frame; a property not taken from the donor is named explicitly.

## Iterating

- Two "not quite" in a row → no third blind round: variants side by side, let the owner point at pixels.
- Never settle on "known limitation" before listing the remaining options.
- Revert a failed CSS experiment at once.
- After each owner note, list every frame it changes and re-render all of them before showing again.
- Name what a static frame cannot show (a missing player, no undo) so the owner rules on it at the board.

## Before handing it over

- Open the rendered mockup and look at it yourself.
- Check sizes and legibility, not only that elements exist. Never shorten approved text without agreement.
- Tables: pinned areas, scrolling, text selection. Dialogs: two compositions — short content and overflow.
- States that read as one window share a shell and fixed anchors; check the transitions at the same window size (height, shared elements, footer, scroll kept).
- A new mode is shown as a sequence: entry → choice → run → result → next launch, stating what is kept, cleared and inherited. A temporarily unavailable action does not erase the selected state.
- State geometry against an explicit anchor ("centre of the first row").
- Colour: a colour lock lists the exact surfaces and the consumers of a shared token that deliberately stay unchanged; check the full matrix palette × lightness × rest/hover/focus/pressed, temporary states included; for filled buttons check text legibility and state distinctness on the real background.
- The UI shows the actual state, the risk and the available action — not explanations of why the data model is "not quite" something; if an explanation is needed, short and in place, preferably on demand.
- A new capability reopens the restrictions it makes pointless: list them and put each to the owner.
- Flag every simplification in the spec (a stub, a skipped state, three items where there will be twenty).
- Take terms from the app's existing i18n keys.

## After the lock

An owner decision that cancels or changes a locked item is written into the board itself, marked and dated, before the next task — the board is what people reopen. Redrawing is optional; naming that the product is now the source of truth for that item is enough.

## Output

The accepted mockup is the design gate; the spec after it can be thin (`sdh-phase-workflow`), and the autonomous mode usually starts here (`sdh-release-lifecycle`).
